   "text": "Only TaTiAlO6 (mp-1217913) is needed to explain the pattern.\n\nThe main observed peaks are at about 19.95, 21.75, 27.45 (strongest), 34.15, 36.1, 39.2, 41.25, 44.35 and 45.3\u00b0 two-theta. I indexed them against the tetragonal trirutile cell of TaTiAlO6 (a = 4.627 \u00c5, c = 8.934 \u00c5).\n\n| Observed (\u00b0) | Assignment | Calculated (\u00b0) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 19.95 (weak) | 002 | 19.86 |\n| 21.75 (strong) | 101 | 21.6 |\n| 27.45 (strongest) | 110 | 27.24 |\n| 34.15 | 112 | 33.9 |\n| 36.1 | 103 | 35.8 |\n| 39.2 | 200 | 38.9 |\n| 41.25 | 113 | 41.0 |\n| 44.35 | 202 | 44.0 |\n| 45.3 | 211 | 44.9 |\n\nEvery calculated position is about 0.8% low. That is consistent with the simulated cell being slightly smaller than the CIF cell, so the order and spacing of the peaks match well. The strong 101 peak is a superlattice reflection, which fits ordering of Ta, Ti and Al along c. I did not index the peaks above 46\u00b0, so that part of the fit is unverified.\n\nThe perovskite candidates are ruled out. A cubic cell near 3.96 \u00c5, as for EuTiO3, would put strong lines near 22.4\u201322.9\u00b0 and 31.9\u201332.6\u00b0 that are not present. CaNdMn2O6 is a similar perovskite cell and fails the same way. K3YV2O8 puts its 101 line at 20.46\u00b0, which misses the strong 21.75\u00b0 peak. No unindexed peaks are left that would call for a second phase.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1217913\"], \"confidence\": 0.85, \"note\": \"Peaks at 19.95, 21.75, 27.45, 34.15, 36.1, 39.2, 41.25 and 44.35-45.3 degrees index as the trirutile 002, 101, 110, 112, 103, 200, 113, 202 and 211 reflections of TaTiAlO6, with no perovskite lines near 32 degrees.\"}",
